                      "questions":  [
                                        {
                                            "q":  "Write a character essay on the speaker of the poem.",
                                            "ans":  "The speaker of \"Confessions of a Born Spectator\" is humorous, honest, cautious, and self-aware. He admires athletes \"with all my heart\" and enjoys watching their heroic deeds. However, he knows that he is not suited for rough competition. He calls his spirit \"limp and bashful\" and gives thanks for his modest physique. His ego may be pleased by the thought of exchanging places with athletes, but prudence wins. He is willing to drink with athletes, eat with them, and buy expensive tickets to watch them, but he refuses to compete. His character teaches that self-knowledge is better than false heroism."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "Explain the theme of common sense versus ego.",
                                            "ans":  "The central theme of the poem is the victory of common sense over ego. The speaker sees athletes scoring, boxing, riding, and performing heroic deeds. Naturally, his ego might enjoy the thought of becoming one of them. Yet he also sees the other side of sports: swollen eyes, gnarled fists, snapped knees, cracked wrists, and doctors being called from the stands. He understands that glory often brings pain. Therefore, when a struggle begins between prudence and ego, prudence wins. Ogden Nash humorously shows that wisdom lies in knowing one\u0027s own limits."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "Explain the significance of sports injuries in the poem.",
                                            "ans":  "Sports injuries are an important image in the poem because they help the speaker justify his choice to remain a spectator. Nash mentions rough athletes who do not consider one another\u0027s feelings. He then gives vivid images such as swollen eyes, gnarled fists, snapped knees, and cracked wrists. These images are comic, but they are also meaningful. They remind us that games may look glorious from the outside, but players often face real pain. The injuries become a symbol of the physical cost of athletic fame. They make the speaker\u0027s cautious choice appear sensible, not cowardly."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "Compare the athlete and the spectator in the poem.",
                                            "ans":  "In the poem, athletes and the spectator are sharply contrasted. Athletes are energetic, zealous, brave, and physically active. They sweat for fun or hire, take the field in gaudy pomp, and perform heroic deeds. However, they also play roughly and suffer injuries. The spectator, on the other hand, is limp, bashful, cautious, and humorous. He admires athletes but does not want to become one of them. He enjoys their heroism from the safety of the stands. While athletes represent action and risk, the spectator represents observation and prudence. Nash makes both sides memorable through humour."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "Do you agree that the speaker is not a coward but a sensible person?",
                                            "ans":  "Yes, the speaker is not a coward but a sensible person. He never says that athletes are foolish or unworthy. In fact, he says, \"With all my heart I do admire\" them. This proves that he respects courage. However, he also understands that sports involve rough play and injuries. He does not pretend to be what he is not. His ego may want glory, but prudence wins. A coward hides fear; the speaker openly admits his preference with humour. His honesty and self-knowledge make him sensible. The poem encourages readers to respect their own nature."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "How does Ogden Nash use humour and sound in the poem?",
                                            "ans":  "Ogden Nash uses humour through exaggeration, playful rhyme, and comic contrast. The speaker admires athletes but describes their world through images of maiming, cracking vertebrae, snapped knees, and doctors in the stands. These details are serious in meaning but funny in presentation. Nash\u0027s rhymes such as jockey/hockey, enter/center, radium/stadium create a light musical effect. He also uses sound words like \"snaps\" and \"cracks\" to make injuries vivid. The speaker\u0027s offer to drink or eat with athletes, but never compete, creates a witty ending. Humour helps Nash teach wisdom without sounding serious or moralising."
                                        },
                                        {
                                            "q":  "How is the poem relevant to modern life?",
                                            "ans":  "The poem is relevant to modern life because people often compare themselves with others and feel pressured to imitate them. Students may admire toppers, athletes, artists, or public speakers and feel that they must become the same. Ogden Nash reminds us that admiration does not require imitation. The speaker respects athletes but knows that he is happier as a spectator. This message is healthy and practical. Everyone has different strengths, interests, and limits. The poem teaches us to celebrate others without jealousy and to choose our own path with honesty, humour, and common sense."
                                        }
